window.products.push({"id":133726,"name":"WR60X10185 Evaporator Fan Motor WR60X10043 AP3875639 PS1019114 WR60X10154 Refrigerator Part Number General Electric Hotpoint WR60X10083 WR60X10068 WR2","category":"homeandappliances","retail price":11.95,"sale price":null,"image":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61h-L36nQzS._AC_SL1436_.jpg","description":"💡 Ge Part Number Wr60x10185 - A high-quality exact equivalent for part numbers AP3875639, 1170107, 197D2039P007, 197D2039P008, AH1019114, EA1019114, PS1019114, WR23X10346, WR23X10350, WR23X10353, WR23X10355, WR23X10364, WR23X10404, WR23X10405, WR23X10406, WR60X10043, WR60X10059, WR60X10068, WR60X10083, WR60X10084, WR60X10149, WR60X10152, WR60X10154, WR60X10157. Evaporator Fan Motor Wr60x10185 is compatible with General Electric and Hotpoint appliances. 🔧 DIY eTips Included - Not sure how to replace the part? Helpful information can be found on the page below. Scroll down to get more repair tips. Acquire valuable skills during the DIY repair project. Wr60x10185 Evaporator Fan Motor For General Electric Refrigerator will help if your appliance is noisy, fridge / freezer are too warm, or it does not start. Ge Wr60x10185 Motor is located in the back of the freezer section. It fits hundreds of models and can be installed in a few minutes. 💡 The Ge Evaporator Fan Motor Wr60x10185 used in many different models of refrigerators. Evaporator Fan Wr60x10185 is responsible for circulating cool air throughout the refrigerator compartment to keep the food fresh. The motor is housed in a plastic casing with two electrical terminals for connecting the wires. Refrigerator Evaporator Fan Motor Wr60x10185 meets or exceeds all manufacturer specifications. We provide a lifetime warranty and guarantee 100% money back. 💡 When the refrigerator is running, the Wr60x10185 Ge Evaporator Fan Motor is powered on, which causes the fan blade to rotate. The rotation of the fan blade creates a flow of air that moves across the evaporator coils. As a result, the evaporator coils are cooled, and the air inside the refrigerator compartment is also cooled. This ensures that the food in the refrigerator is kept at a safe temperature and does not spoil. 💡 How do you test a GE evaporator fan motor? To test a GE evaporator fan motor, you should turn off the power and locate the fan motor. Then remove the back panel, disconnect the wires, and use a multimeter to measure the resistance of the motor. The reading should be between 0 and 50 ohms. Reconnect the wires, turn on the power, and listen for the fan motor to turn on. If it doesn't, it may need to be replaced.","images":["https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61h-L36nQzS._AC_SL1436_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61zNIJh6ZCL._SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61TWUez1VxL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51TIpnkrEpL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51I6jTu8yCL._AC_SL1300_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31o9GyU6jnL._AC_.jpg"],"specifications":{"brand":"PartsBroz","source_asin":"B07MWP9F43","category_path":"Home & Appliances"},"sku":"BM-133726","productType":"Home & Appliances","inventory":0,"age_group":"Adult","gender":"Unisex","source_url":"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07MWP9F43","source_catalog":"Amazon Reviews 2023 product metadata","source_file":"meta_Appliances.jsonl","source_asin":"B07MWP9F43","price_pending":true,"merchant_ready":false});
